{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYQszaBVQbtHWwmzAxW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Maybe several hundred orders in total\" is how DoorDash CEO Tony Xu described what third-party AI agents have sent the company, in answer to an analyst's question on the third-quarter earnings call. That is an executive's estimate on a call. The article doesn't say what period it covers or how DoorDash identifies an order as agent-placed, which limits how much precision the number can carry.\n\nThe scale is still clear. The company's 2025 report put its average at 3 million deliveries a day, and the agents Xu named, such as Muse, Instinct and Fo, are contributing several hundred orders against that. He added that users are still predominantly ordering through DoorDash's app.\n\nThe figure also covers a narrow slice of agent activity. It counts orders arriving from outside agents. DoorDash has its own conversational ordering tools, including Ask DoorDash, which has been available since June and takes orders from prompts and photos, a CLI agent from July and a text-to-order agent from September. The article gives no volume for any of them. So the number says little about how many customers are already ordering through an agent inside DoorDash, and only the outside-agent channel is shown to be small.\n\nXu's own summary was that you never want to fight gravity, but you don't want to be delusional either. Read against the numbers he gave, that sounds like a company treating agent ordering as a direction worth preparing for, with advertising inside agents as one possible future opportunity, while telling investors the volume has not arrived yet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1368,"cache_read_input_tokens":3068,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1368},"output_toke